PAGES.q5_1 = {
  intro: "The component diagram is the next level of detail below the layer diagram: it shows the modules that make up the system and who talks to whom. This page pins down the exact six boxes the Stage 4 checklist expects — five modules inside the API container plus File Storage as a separate container — and the conversations between them, so you can write the Mermaid source offline and let OpenCode render it. It also settles the open questions (Settings/Categories and the LLM provider) before the professor asks.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The six boxes and where they live", b: "The container decision was already made in Step 5 plan Q3: four containers — Client, API Server (with the AI agent merged inside, so stopping the API stops the agent), Database, and File Storage. The component diagram goes one level deeper: it opens the API container and shows its modules. Per TASKS.md Stage 4 and step5-plan Q4, the API container has exactly five modules — Auth, Expenses, Income, AI Agent/Tools, Chat — and File Storage is its own separate container box. That makes six boxes total in the component diagram: five inside the API, plus File Storage next to the Database." },
    { h: "Responsibilities and conversations, one line per component", b: "Auth — register/login, sessions and roles; talks to the API core and the DB (users and roles tables). Expenses — CRUD plus the preview loop (input goes to AI extraction, then confirm/edit/discard); talks to the AI Agent, the DB, and File Storage. Income — CRUD plus balance analysis; talks to the \n\nDB. AI Agent/Tools — extraction prompts, the query tool and the chart tool, and write actions (limits, subcategories); talks to the DB and to the external LLM provider. Chat — conversation state; talks to the AI Agent. File Storage — saves and reads receipt images; called by Expenses." },
    { h: "Settings and Categories: a module or part of Expenses?", b: "The recommendation is to keep Settings/Categories inside Expenses for the MVP. Categories are predefined main categories with AI-generated subcategories (per ALCANCE.md), and the AI suggests a category the user can override — that logic belongs to the expense flow, not to a separate service. A standalone Settings module can be split out later if the team grows; for the diagram, one line in Expenses' responsibility list is enough. The v1 review flagged a Settings module for limits as a possible missing box — the decision here closes that: it stays inside Expenses for now." },
    { h: "Drawing it: Mermaid C4 vs a labeled flowchart", b: "Native C4 support in Mermaid is still marked experimental in the official docs, so a C4Component diagram can render inconsistently across viewers. A labeled flowchart — one subgraph per container, with the five modules as boxes inside the API subgraph and File Storage as its own box — renders reliably everywhere and still satisfies the Stage 7 checklist (responsibility labels on every box, labeled arrows). Put the LLM provider outside the boundary, and give every arrow a label: JSON between modules, SQL to the DB, file path to File Storage, provider API to the LLM. Validate later on mermaid.live or in the IDE viewer." },
    { h: "What the professor checks", b: "The Stage 4 checklist is specific: the six components must be present (Auth, Expenses, Income, AI Agent/Tools, Chat, File Storage), every arrow must carry a label, and the Q3 decision must be respected — the agent is merged inside the API and File Storage is a separate container, not a module. A diagram that draws six modules inside the API box fails both the count and the container model. The correct shape is five modules in one subgraph, plus File Storage as its own box beside the Database, with the Client and the DB as the remaining container boxes at the edges.\n\nstep5-plan Q4 explains why the modules are separate: it lets the team divide the work between colleagues — each module is one folder and one person's responsibility. When you write your one-line-per-component answer, include that reasoning; it turns a diagram question into a teamwork answer. Two arrows deserve special attention: Chat has no direct DB access in the MVP — it always goes through the AI Agent — and Expenses is the only module that touches File Storage. The professor checks exactly these two.\n\nFinally, note what is NOT a component: the LLM provider (external, outside the boundary), the Admin (deliberately excluded per Q1), and Settings/Categories (part of Expenses). Writing the exclusions in your answer shows you understood the boundary, and it pre-empts the 'what about X?' question in the review. The six-box answer plus this exclusion list is the complete Stage 4 response; nothing else is expected in the diagram." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "For each of the six components, write who calls whom and the arrow label (JSON, SQL, file path, provider API).", info: "Auth → DB (SQL); Expenses → AI Agent (JSON) and File Storage (file path); Chat → AI Agent (JSON); AI Agent → DB (SQL) and LLM (provider API)." },
    { q: "Is Settings/Categories a component or part of Expenses?", info: "Part of Expenses for the MVP; a separate module later." },
    { q: "Where does the LLM provider sit — inside or outside the system boundary?", info: "Outside (external entity), per step5-plan Q2 and the Stage 7 checklist." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — Component diagram (pending, repo)",
    "docs/step5-plan.md — Q3/Q4 container and module decisions (repo)",
    "TASKS.md — Step 5 Stage 4 (repo)",
    "Mermaid C4 diagrams — https://mermaid.ai/open-source/syntax/c4.html"
  ]
};
PAGES.q5_2 = {
  intro: "DFD 1 is the register-expense journey drawn as a data flow: every step, every branch, and every error path from the moment the user submits a photo, audio note, or text until the row is saved — or nothing is. It is the heart of the product (the confirm/edit/discard loop) and the diagram the professor will read first. This page gives you the ordered steps, the three preview branches, and the error cases so your diagram is complete offline.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The happy path, in order", b: "The flow comes straight from step5-plan Journey 1 and ALCANCE.md's confirmation flow: (1) the user submits input — receipt photo, audio note, or text; (2) the API stores the file in File Storage (the path is saved); (3) the API calls the AI vision/parse step — or Whisper for audio; (4) the AI returns the structured candidate: store, date, total, items, suggested category; (5) the preview is shown with editable fields; (6a) Confirm → validate → save the receipt and expense rows with user_id; the image file goes to File Storage; (7) the user sees the saved confirmation." },
    { h: "The three branches off the preview node", b: "The preview node must show exactly three labeled branches — that is what Stage 7 checklist item 8 checks. Confirm: validate the fields and save the row (user_id, currency, category, items). Edit: the user changes fields and the flow goes back to the preview (re-preview) — no page navigation. Discard: a confirm dialog, then delete the file and end the flow with nothing saved. All three destinations must be visible in the diagram; a preview node with only a save arrow fails the checklist." },
    { h: "Error branches", b: "Three error cases belong in DFD 1. Unreadable input (blurry receipt, inaudible audio) → fallback message plus manual entry, per the q4_4 answer. AI API down → retry with a friendly message; the user is never left staring at a spinner. Validation fails (negative total, missing date) → reject with a message and return to the preview. ALCANCE.md's pre-save section exists precisely for these edge cases — each one must appear as a branch, because the professor will look for them." },
    { h: "Who owns the file and where the category comes from", b: "File Storage owns the binary; the database stores the path — SCHEMA.md is explicit that receipt.image is a TEXT path, not the binary, to keep the DB light. The API resolves the path when serving the image back to the user. The category comes from the AI suggestion with a user override: the AI proposes a predefined main category and generates a subcategory on the fly, the user can change it before saving, and the confirmed value is stored as the foreign key on the expense row. For audio: Whisper transcribes to text first, then the same parser as the text input — one pipeline, not two." },
    { h: "Validation and the saved row", b: "What exactly gets saved on Confirm? Per SCHEMA.md, one receipt row (store, date, total, currency, payment method, image path, purchase reason) plus one or more expense rows (name, amount, subcategory, item reason). Even a simple 'spent $15 on lunch' creates a minimal receipt with store = 'Manual' and image = null — every expense must belong to a receipt, and the receipt belongs to the user through user_id.\n\nThe DFD should show user_id flowing into every save — the data-scoping rule made visible: the server never returns another user's rows because every row carries its owner. Validation sits before the save: a negative total, a missing date, or an unknown currency is rejected with a message and the flow returns to the preview. If the professor asks 'what stops a corrupted row from entering the DB?', this branch is the answer.\n\nTwo schema notes belong in your answer. Total is stored as printed on the receipt (the truth), even when it differs from the sum of items — taxes and discounts — which is why the preview shows total as its own field. And ownership consistency: Expense.user_id and Receipt.user_id must always reference the same user; the application enforces it, so the DFD should draw the save as one transaction, not two independent writes." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Write step 2 precisely: who owns the file path — the API or the DB?", info: "The DB stores the path (receipt.image); the API resolves it when serving the image." },
    { q: "Where does audio fit — transcribed to text first, then parsed?", info: "Whisper → text → same parser as the text input: one pipeline." },
    { q: "What happens to the file when the user edits — keep or re-upload?", info: "Keep; only re-upload on a new photo." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Processing Flow and Pre-save (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — Receipt and Expense entities (repo)",
    "docs/step5-plan.md — Journey 1 (repo)",
    "Mermaid flowchart docs — https://mermaid.js.org/syntax/flowchart.html"
  ]
};
PAGES.q5_3 = {
  intro: "DFD 2 is the ask-the-AI journey: how a natural-language question becomes a database query, an answer, and optionally a chart. It is the diagram that proves the agent owns the data instead of guessing. This page lists the steps in order, the MVP tool set, and the guardrails that keep every answer honest — every number must come from the database, never from the model's imagination.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The flow, step by step", b: "From step5-plan Journey 2: (1) the user asks in chat, e.g. 'how much did I spend on food this month?'; (2) the Chat module forwards the question to the agent; (3) the agent plans tool calls — query_expenses with filters (category, date range, and user_id always); (4) the query tool runs against the database (SQLAlchemy); (5) the agent formats the answer; (6) if a chart is requested or useful, a chart tool renders it (Chart.js) and the result is attached to the answer; (7) the no-data case returns a friendly message instead of an error." },
    { h: "The MVP tool set", b: "The agent exposes a small set of tools — query_expenses, query_income, make_chart, and maybe get_summary: four to six tools is enough for the MVP. Each tool is a function the model can call with typed arguments; the filters are category, date range, and always user_id. The analytics queries ride on the indexes already defined in SCHEMA.md (expense.user_id, receipt.date, income.date), so 'spending this month' and 'spending by category' are fast, indexed lookups rather than full scans." },
    { h: "Guardrails: data scoping and honesty", b: "Two rules make the agent trustworthy. Data scoping: the agent NEVER queries without user_id — ALCANCE.md states every query is filtered by user_id and the server never returns another user's rows. Honesty: the agent never invents numbers — every figure in an answer comes from the tool result, and charts are generated from query results, never from the model's imagination. When a tool call fails, the failure is returned as an error the agent explains ('the database query failed, try again') instead of a hallucinated answer." },
    { h: "The no-data case", b: "When the query returns nothing, the agent answers with a friendly no-data message plus a suggestion — 'nothing recorded for food this month — try last month, or add an expense'. The suggestion doubles as the next demo step: the user adds an expense, then asks the same question again and sees the answer appear. This case must be drawn in DFD 2 as a branch off the tool result, because it is the most common question a professor will test." },
    { h: "How the answer is built and formatted", b: "The agent formats the answer from the tool result, never from its own memory: the total for the period, optionally a breakdown by category or a comparison with last month, and a chart when it helps. The chart is generated from the query result (Chart.js) and attached to the answer. In DFD 2 this is a branch from the tool result to a chart step and then to the user — the chart never appears out of thin air.\n\nEvery answer can show the tools that produced it — 'queried DB · made chart' — which is both a UI element (q6_4) and a diagram element: the professor can trace any number in the chat back to a box in DFD 2. If a number has no box behind it, the diagram is wrong; that traceability is the point of the flow. Keep the tool list short — query_expenses, query_income, make_chart, get_summary — because each tool is code to review, and the demo only needs these four.\n\nThe interface-agnostic rule applies here too: the same flow runs on web, Telegram, or app; only the presentation of the answer changes. Write the steps once and the interface decision later does not redraw this diagram. The no-data branch ends the flow the same way on every interface — a friendly message plus a suggestion — so it belongs in the diagram, not in the interface mockups." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "List the 4-6 tools the agent will expose in the MVP.", info: "query_expenses, query_income, make_chart, maybe get_summary — that is enough." },
    { q: "What does the agent answer when the user asks something with no data?", info: "Friendly no-data message + suggestion ('try last month, or add an expense')." },
    { q: "Should charts be images or inline HTML?", info: "Inline HTML/SVG renders crisply in a Mini App or web view." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — AI Agent Vision (repo)",
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 2 (6, 7) (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — Indexes for analytics (repo)",
    "Chart.js — https://www.chartjs.org/"
  ]
};
PAGES.q5_4 = {
  intro: "The layer diagram already exists in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; the Stage 7 checklist items 1-5 are about catching drift before the professor does. This page walks each item with the current diagram in hand, so you can report findings offline and hand the fix list to OpenCode, which re-validates after every change.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Checklist item 1: every box has a responsibility label", b: "Each box must describe a responsibility, not a brand: the API box is 'API Server + AI Agent — REST / JSON', the client box is 'Client — Web / Telegram / App (interface TBD)', the database is 'Main DB — TBD candidate: PostgreSQL', and File Storage is 'receipt images — local disk (demo\n\n)'. A box that shows a brand (for example a framework name) instead of a responsibility fails the item. The current layer diagram already follows this pattern — the check is to confirm nothing drifted." },
    { h: "Item 2: every arrow has a label", b: "No naked arrows: each edge carries a protocol or data label. Current arrows: Client → API labeled 'HTTPS / JSON'; API → LLM labeled 'provider API'; API → DB labeled 'SQL / CRUD'; API → File Storage labeled 'upload / read media'. If you find an arrow without a label, write it in the answer — OpenCode fixes it and re-validates. Labels at the diagram level name the protocol or the data moving across the edge, not the function call." },
    { h: "Items 3-4: boundaries and direction", b: "External entities sit outside the system boundary: the user and the LLM provider are both outside — per step5-plan Q2, the provider is an external dependency, exactly like a bank API would \n\nbe. And no bidirectional arrows unless the flow really goes both ways: client → API is one-way at the diagram level (the response is part of the same request/response pair, not a second arrow). If the diagram shows a double-headed arrow where the flow is actually one-way, that is a checklist failure to report." },
    { h: "Item 5: TBD annotations list candidates", b: "Open decisions are drawn as TBD with candidate technologies, not as bare 'TBD'. The known open points from docs/ARCHITECTURE.md: the interface (web / Telegram / app), the tech stack (DB candidate: PostgreSQL), and the price source for the Phase 3 supermarket feature. 'TBD — candidate: PostgreSQL' passes the item; a bare 'TBD' fails it. When you answer, name each TBD you can see in the diagram and whether it lists a candidate — that is the exact report OpenCode needs to close the item." },
    { h: "What to do with findings", b: "Report findings in the same shape as the checklist: item number, pass or fail, and the exact box or arrow that failed — for example 'item 2: the arrow API → LLM has no label'. OpenCode then fixes the diagram and re-validates it; your report is the work order, so vagueness costs a round trip. If everything passes, say so explicitly: 'all five items pass' is a valid, complete answer.\n\nThe review exists to catch drift before the professor does — the diagrams are the contract, marked PROPOSAL until the stack is decided. That means the review checks correctness, not polish: labels, boundaries, direction, and candidate TBDs. Do not over-annotate; every extra label is something else to keep consistent when Step 8 replaces the TBDs with the real stack. A bare 'TBD' is the most common drift: someone replaces 'candidate: PostgreSQL' with 'TBD' while editing, and the item silently fails — look for that specifically.\n\nOne subtlety worth stating: the LLM provider stays outside the boundary even though the agent is inside the API — the provider is rented, not built. That inside/outside split (inside = we build, outside = we rent) is step5-plan Q2, and it is the item professors probe most often. When you cannot open the diagram (phone-only trip), reconstruct it from this page: four boxes, four labeled arrows, one external entity — and mark the rest 'check later', the same rule as q5_5." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Check the layer diagram: any box or arrow without a label?", info: "List them here; OpenCode fixes and re-validates." },
    { q: "Is the LLM provider correctly OUTSIDE the boundary?", info: "Yes per Q2 — it is an external dependency, like a bank API would be." },
    { q: "Do the TBD annotations list candidates, not just 'TBD'?", info: "'TBD — candidate: PostgreSQL' passes; bare 'TBD' fails the checklist." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — Layer diagram (repo)",
    "docs/step5-plan.md — Q2 and Q7 answers (repo)",
    "TASKS.md — Step 5 Stage 7 checklist (repo)",
    "Mermaid flowchart docs — https://mermaid.js.org/syntax/flowchart.html"
  ]
};
PAGES.q5_5 = {
  intro: "The remaining Stage 7 checklist items (6-8, plus the two open questions) are binary — you can tick them now so OpenCode only renders. This page tells you exactly what each item checks, what answering 'from memory' looks like on a phone-only trip, and how to report uncertainty honestly: 'check later' is a valid answer, because the agent re-runs the full checklist anyway.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Item 6: all diagrams render with zero warnings", b: "This item happens online — paste each Mermaid diagram into mermaid.live or the IDE viewer and check the console for warnings. If you are phone-only during the trip, answer from memory and mark 'check later': the OpenCode prompt runs the checklist and reports pass/fail per item regardless of your answer. The repo rule from opencode-prompt.md is that every Mermaid diagram is validated before saving, so a broken render should never reach the docs." },
    { h: "Item 7: file storage container present, receipt.image path flows to it", b: "The File Storage container must exist in the diagrams with the receipt image path flowing to it. In the layer diagram the arrow is API → File Storage labeled 'upload / read media'. SCHEMA.md confirms the data contract: receipt.image stores a path (TEXT), not the binary — the image file lives on disk, the DB stays light. Check that the arrow label matches this contract, and that the component diagram (q5_1) repeats it: Expenses → File Storage with a file path label." },
    { h: "Item 8: the confirmation loop is present in DFD 1", b: "The preview node in DFD 1 must show three labeled branches: confirm (save the row), edit (change fields, re-preview), and discard (delete the file, nothing saved). This loop is the heart of the product, so it is the one thing the checklist explicitly names. If a branch is missing — for example only a save arrow out of the preview — that is a finding to report, and OpenCode patches the diagram and re-validates it." },
    { h: "The two open questions and what the diagrams miss", b: "Question (3) asks for any box or arrow you cannot label; question (4) asks what the diagrams miss entirely. The v1 review example is a Settings module for spending limits — decided in q5_1 to be part of Expenses for the MVP, so the honest note is 'covered inside Expenses', not a new box. Other candidates: a Whisper/audio step box in DFD 1, or the fallback-manual-entry branch. Whatever you cannot verify offline, write 'check later' — the agent re-runs the checklist and reports per item." },
    { h: "What 'check later' means downstream", b: "When you answer 'check later', the OpenCode prompt still runs the full Stage 7 checklist and reports pass/fail per item — your marks become the agent's action list, so nothing is lost by being honest. Inventing a pass would be caught by the render validation anyway, and it would put a wrong diagram in the docs. Honest uncertainty is the workflow's design assumption; the trip is explicitly the place for it. The render check (item 6) is the only item that truly needs a screen; the other three are reading checks you can do from the docs.\n\nThe chain that follows: your answers finalize docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — component diagram, DFD 1, DFD 2, all marked PROPOSAL — which ticks the Step 5 checkboxes in TASKS.md, which unlocks the Step 7 mockups. A 'check later' item delays only that item, not the chain: put it at the end of your answer so the agent processes the confirmed items first.\n\nKeep the checklist answers in the trip export (text or audio). The daily JSON export from TRIP-GUIDE.md is the deliverable the post-trip run reads, so an answer recorded today survives a dead phone tomorrow. The v1 review example — a Settings module for limits — shows the shape of a good 'anything missing?' answer: name the candidate, state the decision (part of Expenses for the MVP), and stop. Three sentences per item is enough." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "From memory: does DFD 1 show the three branches off the preview?", info: "If unsure, write 'check later' — the agent re-runs the checklist." },
    { q: "Anything the diagrams miss entirely (a new box, an arrow)?", info: "Example from the v1 review: a Settings module for limits." }
  ],
  links: [
    "TASKS.md — Step 5 Stage 7 checklist (repo)",
    "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — Layer diagram and Data flow (repo)",
    "docs/guides/architecture-diagrams-guide.md — diagram rules (repo)",
    "mermaid.live editor — https://mermaid.live/"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_1 = {
  intro: "The upload screen is the app's front door and the demo's opening shot — the professor forms a first impression in the first ten seconds. This page defines the three inputs, the loading state, and the preview card so you can describe the screen in words now and let OpenCode build docs/mockups/upload.html later. The goal is one obvious action per input and a preview that appears the moment the AI finishes.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Three inputs, one obvious action each", b: "The screen has three clearly separated inputs: photo (a camera button), voice (a mic button), and text (a field). Each input has one obvious action — tap the camera to shoot a receipt, tap the mic to say 'spent $15 on lunch at Subway', type into the text field for manual entry. The photo path should show a thumbnail preview before sending, so the user sees exactly what the AI will read. Layout is mobile-first: one column, big touch targets, the camera action right under the thumb." },
    { h: "Anti-patterns that kill the demo", b: "The classic failures: cramming all inputs into one ambiguous 'add' button (nobody knows what happens next); making the user type the category manually (the AI suggests it — that is the product's value); hiding the preview behind an extra click; and giving no feedback while the AI processes. Each anti-pattern is a question the professor can ask, so the answer to 'why is this screen good?' is: one action per input, AI-suggested category, preview immediately visible, feedback while processing." },
    { h: "The preview card after extraction", b: "After the AI extracts, an editable summary card appears — store, date, total, category, items. This card IS the start of the review screen (q6_2): every field is editable before saving, and the user chooses confirm, edit, or discard. While the AI is still processing, the card area shows a skeleton or spinner with 'Reading your receipt…' — a small line that sets expectations and prevents double-taps. The transition from upload to review is seamless because they are one flow, not two screens." },
    { h: "After confirm: where does the user land?", b: "Landing on the list with the new expense highlighted is the classic pattern: the user sees the result of their action immediately and can keep entering expenses in the same session. A green check or toast confirms the save, and the list refreshes with the new row highlighted. This keeps the loop fast for repeated entries — the demo can show three receipts in a row without the professor waiting on navigation." },
    { h: "The demo script for this screen", b: "The screen is also the demo's opening move, so script it: tap the camera, shoot a receipt, watch the skeleton ('Reading your receipt…'), see the editable card appear, tap confirm, and land on the list with the new expense highlighted. That loop is under thirty seconds and it shows the entire product: input, AI extraction, human confirmation, storage. Rehearse it until the timing is natural — the professor's first question is usually 'can I try?'\n\nPrepare the second move too: a deliberately crumpled or blurry receipt. It produces a low-confidence extraction, which demonstrates the 'AI read this — verify the total' hint and the edit path — the 'what happens when the AI is wrong?' answer in action. A demo that only shows the happy path invites that question; a demo that shows the recovery answers it before it is asked. Have the fallback ready: if the AI misreads the total, the user corrects it inline and confirms — the flow never requires deleting and re-entering.\n\nKeep the browser window sized like a phone (one column, big touch targets) even on a laptop — the mockup is mobile-first and the demo should look like the product, not like a desktop page squeezed into a phone frame. Note the decision in your answer: three separate inputs beat one ambiguous 'add' button, because each input has one obvious action. Write the input order (photo + mic as big buttons, text field below) so the mockup builder does not have to guess." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Sketch the screen: order of the three inputs top to bottom.", info: "Photo + mic as big buttons, text field below — or a single input bar with icons." },
    { q: "What does the preview card show while the AI is still processing?", info: "A skeleton/spinner with 'Reading your receipt…' sets expectations." },
    { q: "After confirm, where does the user land — list, dashboard, or a success state?", info: "Landing on the list with the new expense highlighted is the classic pattern." }
  ],
  links: [
    "TASKS.md — Step 7 screen #1 (repo)",
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Input Methods and Pre-save (repo)",
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 1 (1-3) (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_2 = {
  intro: "The review/confirm screen carries the whole pre-save contract from ALCANCE.md: nothing touches the database until the user says Confirm. It is also your answer to the professor's 'what happens when the AI is wrong?' question — the answer is: the user sees the extraction, edits it, or discards it, before anything is saved. This page locks the five decisions that make or break the screen.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The fields and the pre-save contract", b: "The screen shows the extracted fields — store, date, total (currency-aware), category (a dropdown of predefined categories with the AI suggestion preselected), and items (collapsible detail). The contract from ALCANCE.md pre-save: Confirm saves to the DB, Edit changes fields and re-previews, Discard deletes everything and nothing is saved. The schema behind it is the Receipt entity (store, date, total, currency, image path) plus Expense rows (name, amount, subcategory, reason) — one receipt can carry several expense items." },
    { h: "Edit: inline, not a new page", b: "Editing happens inline in the preview, with a re-preview after the user saves the changes — no page navigation. Inline keeps the confirm/edit/discard loop on one screen, which is the fastest possible flow for the demo and the least code to build. The file is kept on edit; a new photo is only uploaded when the user takes one. If editing were a separate page, the loop would feel like three screens and the demo would slow down exactly where the professor is watching." },
    { h: "Discard is destructive: confirm it", b: "Discard deletes everything — the expense and the uploaded file (per the q4_4 answer, discard-deletes-file). Destructive actions always get a confirmation dialog: 'Delete this expense? This can't be undone.' with Cancel and Delete buttons. The dialog is one line of UI and it prevents the demo's worst moment: the professor tapping the wrong button and losing data on screen. Nothing is saved on discard, and the flow ends cleanly." },
    { h: "Confirm, success feedback, and AI confidence", b: "Confirm shows a green check and the expense appears in the list (optimistic UI) or a toast plus redirect — the classic pattern. The AI-confidence detail completes the screen: when confidence is low, a subtle hint appears — 'AI read this — verify the total' — because total and date are the highest-risk fields on a receipt. This makes the 'AI is wrong' case visible and fast to fix, which is exactly the answer the professor wants to see when they test with a crumpled receipt." },
    { h: "What the professor will ask", b: "The question this screen exists to answer is 'what happens when the AI is wrong?' — and the answer is visible in the screen itself: a preview the user can edit, a discard that deletes everything, and a low-confidence hint that points at the riskiest fields. Make that answer fast: inline edit, one dialog for discard, one tap for confirm. A review screen that requires two clicks per field fails the demo. The pre-save contract from ALCANCE.md is the source: nothing reaches the database until Confirm — state that sentence in your answer.\n\nPrepare the edge cases: a blurry receipt (low confidence, verify hint), a wrong currency (inline currency picker), a missing store name (optional field, or a default), and an AI API failure (fallback to manual entry, per q4_4). Every edge case ends in one of the three branches — confirm, edit, discard — which is exactly the contract the diagram in q5_2 draws. Note which fields are currency-aware: the receipt stores its purchase currency per SCHEMA.md and converts on display.\n\nThe success feedback matters less than the state after it: landing on the list with the new row highlighted closes the loop visually. Write the discard dialog text in your answer ('Delete this expense? This can't be undone.'), because the exact wording is the kind of detail the mockup builder should not invent. Also decide what Edit keeps: the uploaded file stays; a new photo is only uploaded when the user takes one." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Which field gets the 'verify me' highlight when AI confidence is low?", info: "Total and date are the highest-risk fields on a receipt." },
    { q: "Edit: modal, inline form, or new page?", info: "Inline keeps the loop on one screen — fastest for the demo." },
    { q: "Write the discard confirm dialog text.", info: "'Delete this expense? This can't be undone.' + Cancel/Delete." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Pre-save (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — Receipt and Expense entities (repo)",
    "TASKS.md — Step 7 screen #2 (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_3 = {
  intro: "The dashboard sells the demo: three summary cards and one chart answer 'am I on track this month?' at a glance. This page fixes the exact widgets, the queries behind them, and the empty state. The rule: every widget must be tied to a real question the professor might ask — a widget without a question is decoration, and decoration is review surface.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Three cards, one chart — and nothing else", b: "The strongest card candidates: spent this month (total), top category (with its percent of total), and income vs expenses (the balance). The one chart is either spending by week (a line chart showing the trend) or spending by category (a bar chart showing the breakdown) — pick the one that answers the demo question 'am I on track this month?', which favors the weekly trend. Every extra widget is more code, more review work, and a new place for the demo to break." },
    { h: "The queries behind the widgets", b: "Each card maps to a concrete query so nothing on screen is decorative: spent this month is 'SELECT SUM(total) FROM receipt WHERE user_id \n\n= ? AND date >= month_start'; top category groups by category with a percentage; income vs expenses joins the Income table, which exists precisely for balance analysis per ALCANCE.md. These analytics ride the indexes already locked in SCHEMA.md — receipt.user_id, receipt.date, income.date — so the queries are fast even as data grows." },
    { h: "One source of truth: the DB", b: "Chart.js renders from the same query results the agent uses, so the dashboard and the chat answers never disagree — the database is the single source of truth. This is the same rule as DFD 2: charts are generated from query results, never from the model's imagination. If the dashboard said one number and the chat said another, the professor would ask which one is real; with one source there is no second number." },
    { h: "The empty state", b: "When there is no data yet, the dashboard shows a friendly 'Add your first expense' call to action — never a blank page. The empty state covers the balance card too: with no income registered, the card explains that income registration exists instead of showing a confusing zero. The empty state is also the demo's opening move: the professor opens the app, sees the CTA, taps it, and the whole loop starts from the upload screen." },
    { h: "The dashboard and the demo story", b: "The dashboard tells the demo's story arc: it opens on the empty state ('Add your first expense'), fills as expenses land, and ends with the three cards answering 'am I on track this month?' — spent, top category, balance. The chart (weekly trend) shows the same data moving, which is the visual payoff. Every widget maps to a question the professor can ask, so every widget has a ready answer. The cards are candidates, not laws: pick the three that match the demo data you will show — a month with clear food spending makes the top-category card interesting.\n\nCross-checking is part of the story: the numbers on the dashboard must equal the numbers the chat agent reports (q6_4). Because both render from the same query results, a professor who asks 'is this consistent with the chat?' gets a one-line answer: same database, same query, same number. The same rule applies to the AI-generated chart in chat: it comes from the tool result, never from the model's imagination (q5_3). Write that sentence in your answer — it is the design's strongest defense.\n\nKeep the widget count at three cards and one chart, and name the queries behind them (SUM(total) by month, category percentages, income vs expenses). If the professor proposes an extra widget — 'add a savings card' — the correct answer is: which question does it answer, and does the data exist? A widget with no question is decoration. The empty state covers both expenses and income: with no income registered, the balance card explains itself instead of showing a confusing zero." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Write the exact 3 cards and their queries.", info: "e.g. 'Spent this month: SELECT SUM(total) WHERE user_id=? AND date >= month_start'." },
    { q: "Which chart answers the demo story best — trend or breakdown?", info: "Trend shows behavior over time; breakdown shows categories at a glance." },
    { q: "What is the empty state when there is no data yet?", info: "A friendly 'Add your first expense' CTA — never a blank page." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 2 (8) (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — Receipt and Income indexes (repo)",
    "Chart.js — https://www.chartjs.org/"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_4 = {
  intro: "The chat screen is where the agent shows off: natural-language questions, real tool calls, inline charts, and a 'tools used' line that makes agentic work visible. This page defines the screen's elements, the suggestion chips that seed the demo, and the error handling that keeps the demo safe when the agent cannot answer.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The core elements", b: "The screen has three parts: the message list (user bubbles and AI bubbles), the input box (text, mic optional), and suggestion chips ('This month?', 'By category?', 'vs last month?') that seed the demo with one tap. Charts render inline inside AI bubbles — inline HTML/SVG works in a Telegram Mini App or a web view and scales cleanly at any size, unlike image files that need storage and resizing. Keep the input simple: one text box, mic optional." },
    { h: "Make the agent's work visible", b: "Under each AI answer, a small 'tools used' line — for example 'queried DB · made chart' — shows the tool calls behind the answer. Professors love watching agentic work, and it is one line of \n\nUI. The line is also honest: it tells the user (and the evaluator) that the answer came from a database query, not from the model guessing. This connects the screen to DFD 2, where the tool calls are drawn." },
    { h: "Suggestion chips are the demo's training wheels", b: "Chips make the demo deterministic: the professor taps one and it works, every time. Three chips are enough: 'This month?' (date-range filter), 'By category?' (category breakdown), and 'vs last month?' (comparison). Each chip maps to a tool call the agent can already handle with query_expenses filters, so the chip never produces the no-data error in front of an audience. Chips are also the seed list for the empty chat state — never start the demo with a blank screen." },
    { h: "Error handling and chat history", b: "When the agent cannot answer — no data, or a tool failure — the screen shows a friendly message plus a chip suggestion instead of a raw error ('the database query failed, try again'). Raw errors are the one thing that can sink the demo, so every failure path ends in something tappable. Chat history is listed in FEATURES.md as Phase 3 feature 18 — the MVP decision is to keep the conversation in memory only, and let the schema grow a chat_messages table later if the feature ships." },
    { h: "Demo flow and failure-proofing", b: "Script the chat demo in three taps: 'This month?' → an answer with the tools-used line; 'By category?' → an answer with an inline bar chart; 'vs last month?' → a comparison answer. Each tap maps to one tool call, and the chips guarantee the professor never has to type. Keep the input box visible and simple — one text field, mic optional — so the demo can also show a typed question; a typed question shows the natural-language path, with the agent planning a tool call from it.\n\nFailure-proof the screen: the agent cannot answer with no data (friendly message + chip suggestion), a tool failure returns 'the database query failed, try again', and a chart request with no rows returns the no-data message instead of an empty chart. Decide the chat history question in your answer: in-memory for the MVP, with a chat_messages table as the Phase 3 growth path (feature 18). Inline HTML/SVG charts need no file storage and scale at any size — the chart decision (inline, not images) belongs in the answer too.\n\nThe tools-used line ('queried DB · made chart') is the screen's signature: it makes the agentic work visible and it connects the chat to DFD 2, where the tool calls are drawn. Write the three chips and their expected tool calls in your answer — chips, tools, and diagram must name the same four tools (query_expenses, query_income, make_chart, get_summary). One raw error on screen is enough to sink the demo; the friendly-message path is the rule, not the exception." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Write 3 suggestion chips for the demo.", info: "Chips are the demo's training wheels — the professor taps one and it works." },
    { q: "Charts in chat: image files or inline HTML?", info: "Inline HTML/SVG scales cleanly and needs no file storage." },
    { q: "Chat history: keep in memory (MVP) or persist (Phase 3, feature 18)?", info: "In-memory for MVP; the schema can grow a chat_messages table later." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 2 (6, 7) + Phase 3 (18) (repo)",
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — AI Agent Vision (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — data scoping and indexes (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_5 = {
  intro: "Settings stays tiny: a category editor and a monthly limit per category — nothing else. The subtle part is the data model: the limits table must serve both the settings UI and the chat agent (Phase 3, feature 16), so it lives in its own table with an exact shape. This page fixes the editor, the predefined categories, and the exact columns of spending_limits.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The minimal MVP: categories + limits", b: "The settings screen has two sections. The category editor lists the predefined main categories with add, edit, and delete. The spending-limits section has a monthly limit input per category. Subcategories are AI-generated on the fly per ALCANCE.md, so the editor only touches main categories — the user never manages subcategories by hand. Resist everything else: themes, exports, profiles, language settings — each one is review surface and demo risk." },
    { h: "The predefined categories", b: "The MVP ships with Food, Transport, Entertainment, Housing, Health, and Other — the examples ALCANCE.md uses for main categories. The AI suggests a main category and generates a subcategory from the purchase analysis; the user can override the suggestion before saving. Delete semantics matter: deleting a category that already has expenses must either be blocked or reassign the expenses — never orphan them, because the schema requires every expense to belong to a subcategory (and subcategory to category)." },
    { h: "The spending_limits table, exactly", b: "Per SCHEMA.md, the SpendingLimit entity has these columns: user_id (FK, required), name (required label like 'Food budget'), amount (required, in the user's preferred currency), period (required — 'weekly' or 'monthly'), and a target: category_id XOR subcategory_id. The XOR rule means exactly one of the two must be set — never both, never neither — enforced by the UI and by a database CHECK constraint. This is why limits are their own table and not columns on the user or the category: a limit is one rule with one target." },
    { h: "One table, two writers", b: "Two features write to the same table. In the MVP, the settings UI creates limits; in Phase 3, feature 16 lets the agent create and edit limits from chat ('keep eating out under $50/month\n\n'). Both writers must hit the same table so the UI and the agent always agree on the current limits — the agent reads the same data it reports on. ALCANCE.md frames limits as AI-assisted awareness, not hard blocks: the agent tracks and reports progress toward the limit." },
    { h: "What the professor will ask about limits", b: "The likely question is 'how are limits enforced?' — the answer is: they are not hard blocks. ALCANCE.md defines spending limits as AI-assisted awareness: the user states a preference ('keep eating out under $50/month'), the agent tracks spending against it and reports progress. The UI shows the limit and the progress; nothing stops the user from exceeding it. Say that sentence in your answer; it matches the docs and it pre-empts the 'what if I exceed?' question. The agent also reports progress in chat ('you have used 80% of your food limit') — the same table feeds both.\n\nThe second question is 'why a table instead of a column on user or category?' — because a limit is one rule with one target: the XOR rule (category_id XOR subcategory_id, exactly one set) cannot be expressed as a column, and two writers (the settings UI and the chat agent, feature 16) need one shared place. The CHECK constraint in schema.sql enforces the XOR at the database level, so the UI cannot create an ambiguous limit.\n\nThe third is 'what happens when a category is deleted?' — block the deletion or reassign its expenses, never orphan them. Note the limits currency too: amounts are stored in the user's preferred currency, because a limit is a personal target, not a purchase. And decide the editor scope: only main categories are editable; subcategories are AI-generated (ALCANCE.md) and never appear in the settings list. Three answers, all grounded in SCHEMA.md and ALCANCE.md — write them in your answer now." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Which categories ship predefined in the MVP?", info: "Food, Transport, Entertainment, Housing, Health, Other — ALCANCE's examples (extended)." },
    { q: "Delete semantics for a category with existing expenses?", info: "Block deletion or reassign expenses — never orphan them." },
    { q: "Where does the limits table live in your schema, and what are its exact columns?", info: "SpendingLimit in SCHEMA.md: user_id, name, amount, period, category_id XOR subcategory_id." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — SpendingLimit entity (repo)",
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 3 (10, 16) (repo)",
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Categories and Spending Limits (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_6 = {
  intro: "AI-DLC (AWS, 2025) is the lifecycle your AGENTS.md implements — the methodology you will explain to the professor. This page gives you the three phases in your own words, where you review and approve, and the one-sentence mental model that answers 'what did you actually do in this project?'.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The three phases in your own words", b: "Inception is scope and requirements: the agent asks clarifying questions (the question gate — it STOPS until you answer), then produces a plan with work units that you approve or adjust. Construction is the per-unit loop: the AI generates code plus tests, builds and runs them, and you review — approve moves to the next unit, reject loops back to the AI. Operations is deployment: Dockerfile, docker-compose, CI, and docs, which you verify and run. REFERENCES.md Phase 1-3 documents this exact structure, and AGENTS.md implements it in this repo." },
    { h: "Gates vs reviews", b: "A gate is a workflow stop: the agent cannot proceed until you provide input — the clarifying questions in Inception and the approval after each unit. A review is you judging a delivered artifact against the spec. The distinction matters because it explains the agent's behavior: it never passes a gate on its own, and it never skips a review. This is what the AWS quote 'AI defers critical decisions to humans' means in practice — the machine does the volume, the human owns the decisions." },
    { h: "Where testing lives", b: "Testing lives in Construction: the AI writes tests per unit, then a Build and Test run executes after the unit's code is generated. Your review checklist from REFERENCES.md includes 'do the tests actually test something?' — a test that always passes is worse than no test. In Operations, the same discipline applies to deployment artifacts: verify paths and environment variables, then run docker compose up -d and check it works." },
    { h: "Your role: architect and gatekeeper, never typist", b: "REFERENCES.md's core argument: now that AI writes code, your primary job shifts from writing lines to designing, specifying, and reviewing — the shift from implementer to orchestrator documented by the 2026 industry reports it cites (Anthropic 2026, the 2026 CS Playbook). The mental model for the professor: 'we adopted a methodology where the team specifies, the AI implements, and the human approves every unit at quality gates.' Your deliverables: in Inception, answers and approvals; in Construction, review verdicts; in Operations, the deploy approval." },
    { h: "Connecting AIDLC to your evidence", b: "The methodology only convinces if you can point at the artifacts each phase produced. Inception: the scope docs (docs/ALCANCE.md, FEATURES.md), the schema (SCHEMA.md), and the architecture plan (step5-plan.md) — all produced before any code. Construction: the per-unit review records and audit.md logs, plus the code itself. Operations: the Dockerfile, compose file, CI, and README. Your answer should map each phase to a folder or file the professor can open. REFERENCES.md lists the 2026 sources (Anthropic, CS Playbook, Pragmatic Engineer, ACM) that document the implementer-to-orchestrator shift — cite at least one by name.\n\nThe gate behavior is your best evidence that the workflow is real: the agent stops and asks, you answer, it proceeds. If a professor asks 'did the AI do everything?', the answer is: the AI generated the implementation, but every unit passed through my review at a gate — approve or reject — and the rejections went back for fixes. That is the AWS 'AI defers critical decisions to humans' principle in action. The review checklist from REFERENCES.md (does it do exactly what the spec says? is it secure? do the tests test something?) is the Construction gate in one list.\n\nFinally, connect the phase names to the trip itself: the questions you are answering right now are Inception artifacts — they become the AIDLC requirement answers (q6_7). One line in your answer — 'my trip answers pre-fill the Inception gate' — closes the loop between the offline tool and the methodology. A one-line-per-phase summary is enough: the professor wants to hear YOU explain it, not a recitation." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Explain the difference between a gate and a review.", info: "Gate = workflow stops until input; review = you judge a delivered artifact." },
    { q: "Where does testing live in AIDLC?", info: "Construction: AI writes tests per unit; Build and Test runs after all units." },
    { q: "What is YOUR deliverable in each phase?", info: "Inception: answers + approvals. Construction: review verdicts. Operations: deploy approval." }
  ],
  links: [
    "REFERENCES.md — AIDLC phases (repo)",
    "AWS AI-DLC — https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/ai-driven-development-life-cycle",
    "AGENTS.md — the workflow contract (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_7 = {
  intro: "OpenCode is the AI coding agent (CLI/TUI) that will build this project, and AGENTS.md is the contract it follows the moment it starts in the repo. This page explains the startup sequence, the question format, and the gate behavior so your Step 6 notes are accurate enough to survive the professor's questions.",
  sections: [
    { h: "What OpenCode is", b: "OpenCode is an AI coding agent you run inside the repo — a CLI/TUI tool that reads the repository context and executes coding tasks: editing files, running commands, and reporting back. It is the tool the whole trip workflow is built on: trip/opencode-prompt.md is a master prompt that turns your trip answers into repo updates, and TRIP-GUIDE.md shows the exact command to run it. Two modes matter: the interactive TUI for iterative sessions (run it in a pseudo-terminal), and opencode run for bounded one-shot tasks." },
    { h: "What AGENTS.md does at startup", b: "When OpenCode starts in this repo, AGENTS.md is the contract: it loads the AIDLC workflow, resolves the rule-detail files in order (.aidlc/aidlc-rules/, then .aidlc-rule-details/, then .kiro/, then .amazonq/ — the first that exists wins), and asks clarifying questions in a fixed format with [Answer]: tags and multiple-choice options A/B/C/D + X (Other). Your answers are written into aidlc-docs/inception/requirements/requirement-verification-questions.md, every interaction is logged to audit.md, and the workflow STOPS at gates until you approve." },
    { h: "The question format", b: "The format is '[Answer]: tags, A/B/C/D + X (Other)' — each question carries the [Answer]: tag so answers can be matched back, and the options are A/B/C/D plus X for (Other). The trip's Section 4 answers exist precisely to pre-fill this file: when the agent starts, it finds the answers already written instead of re-asking you at the gate. Writing answers in the same format keeps the file consistent and lets the agent parse it mechanically." },
    { h: "Non-interactive runs and permissions", b: "In non-interactive mode, opencode run auto-rejects permission prompts — the agent will not execute a command it was not explicitly allowed to run, which is a safety feature, not a bug. For scripted builds, pass --auto to pre-approve the run, and keep every referenced file inside the repo so the agent can read it. The one-shot build command from TRIP-GUIDE.md is: opencode run \"$(cat trip/opencode-prompt.md)\" — run from the repo root, with the exported trip JSON in trip/. The --auto flag is the difference between a scripted build that finishes unattended and a run that stalls at the first permission prompt, so decide per run whether you want review or automation." },
    { h: "Common pitfalls", b: "Pitfall one: answering the agent's questions with prose instead of the [Answer]: tag format — the answers file becomes inconsistent and the agent cannot match answers to questions. The correct format is '[Answer]: tags, A/B/C/D + X (Other)' — the (Other) belongs with the X option, with no stray parentheses — and the tags let the workflow pre-fill your Section 4 answers. Pitfall two: writing answers or files outside the repo (a local note, a chat window) — OpenCode only sees files inside the repository, so the answer never reaches requirement-verification-questions.md.\n\nPitfall three: running the interactive TUI without a pseudo-terminal and wondering why it misbehaves — the TUI needs pty; one-shot runs (opencode run) are for bounded tasks and auto-reject permissions unless you pass --auto. Pitfall four: expecting the agent to guess at a gate — it stops by design; the correct move is to answer, not to restart it. The gate is not a crash: the agent prints its questions and waits, and that is the workflow's design — the professor will ask about it.\n\nPitfall five: forgetting that AGENTS.md resolves rule-detail files from the first existing path (.aidlc/aidlc-rules/, .aidlc-rule-details/, .kiro/, .amazonq/) — if the project moves, the path order matters. And the trip-specific one: pasting the master prompt without attaching the trip JSON (opencode -f trip/finance-ai-trip-answers-YYYYMMDD.json) — the prompt has nothing to process. Keep the exported JSON in trip/ with a date-stamped name, per TRIP-GUIDE.md, so the run always knows which file to read." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "What does the GATE look like when the agent is waiting?", info: "It stops and prints questions — it is not stuck; answer and it proceeds." },
    { q: "Where do your answers go?", info: "Directly into requirement-verification-questions.md with [Answer]: tags." },
    { q: "One-shot vs interactive: when to use opencode run vs the TUI?", info: "run = bounded tasks; TUI = iterative sessions (use pty)." }
  ],
  links: [
    "OpenCode docs — https://opencode.ai/docs",
    "AGENTS.md — the workflow contract (repo)",
    "trip/opencode-prompt.md — interviewer-mode prompt (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_8 = {
  intro: "Antigravity is Google's agentic development platform — an agentic IDE family with a CLI and SDK — and TASKS.md Step 6 lists it as a tool to research. This page separates what is verified from what you still need to research, and gives you the honest-answer template plus the concrete questions to park for later.",
  sections: [
    { h: "What Antigravity is (verified)", b: "Antigravity is Google's agentic development platform, verified live at antigravity.google: the site describes it as 'our agentic development platform, allowing anyone to build in the agent-first era'. The product family includes the Antigravity IDE (a fully-featured agentic IDE with an agent manager and artifacts), the Antigravity CLI (a terminal-first surface for autonomous coding agents), and the Antigravity SDK (a Python harness for prototyping custom agents). It belongs to the same category as OpenCode and Claude Code: an agent that reads repository context and executes coding tasks." },
    { h: "How it fits this project", b: "For this project, Antigravity is an alternative cockpit to OpenCode — the AIDLC rules in AGENTS.md are designed to be tool-agnostic, so the same workflow can run there. It is not required: OpenCode is the primary tool that will build this project, and Antigravity is the alternative to evaluate. The decision question for the demo is simple — which cockpit do you learn deeply? Pick one primary; the other becomes the fallback." },
    { h: "The honest answer is valid", b: "If you have never used Antigravity, 'I need to research this' is the correct Step 6 answer — TASKS.md asks you to understand the tool, not to have shipped with \n\nit. The trip is exactly where you write that honest understanding down, plus 2-3 concrete questions: does it read AGENTS.md rule files the same way OpenCode does? which models does it use? does it run locally or in the cloud? Those questions become rows in your Step 6 research list (q6_10)." },
    { h: "IDE vs agentic IDE", b: "A classic IDE (VS Code, JetBrains) is a text editor with tooling: it does exactly what you tell it, keystroke by keystroke, and every change is yours. An agentic IDE can plan and execute multi-file changes from natural-language instructions — you describe the goal and the agent edits the codebase, runs commands, and iterates. The practical consequence for this project: the tool you choose determines whether 'the AI implements, you review' (the REFERENCES.md model) happens inside one window or across two." },
    { h: "How to research it when online", b: "Turn 'I need to research this' into a short plan: download Antigravity, open a small repository (this one works), and test the basics — does it read AGENTS.md? does it follow the rule-detail resolution order? which models does it offer? Run one real task (for example, rendering a Mermaid diagram) and note how it behaves compared with OpenCode. The verified facts are already here: antigravity.google describes the platform as agent-first, with the agentic IDE, a terminal-first CLI, and a Python SDK for custom agents — use those as the baseline.\n\nThe second research question is operational: does it run locally or in the cloud, and what does that mean for your machine and your data? The third is practical: pricing and limits for the free tier, since the project has no budget. Each answer is one paragraph in your Step 6 notes; the goal is being able to say three accurate sentences about Antigravity, not mastering it. Screenshots and one saved link in the notes make the research verifiable later.\n\nWhat counts as 'understood' for TASKS.md: you can explain what Antigravity is (Google's agentic development platform — IDE, CLI, SDK), how it compares with OpenCode, and whether it fits this project. That is the Step 6 row ticked. If the tool changed by then, say so — the research question list is a living document, not a contract. And write the honest baseline in your answer today: 'I have not used it; my research questions are X, Y, Z' — that is a complete Step 6 answer by itself." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "What is the difference between an IDE and an agentic IDE?", info: "An agentic IDE can plan and execute multi-file changes from natural language." },
    { q: "Which of your tools (OpenCode, Antigravity) will be the PRIMARY cockpit?", info: "Pick one primary to learn deeply; the other becomes the fallback." },
    { q: "Write 2 questions about Antigravity to research online.", info: "e.g. 'Does Antigravity read AGENTS.md rule files?'" }
  ],
  links: [
    "TASKS.md — Step 6 (repo)",
    "Google Antigravity — https://antigravity.google/"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_9 = {
  intro: "Docker is how the professor runs your project without 'it works on my machine' excuses. This page explains the four terms Step 6 expects — container, Dockerfile, docker-compose, volumes — and the exact commands the demo needs. The one sentence to remember: with Docker, one command must be enough to run the whole project.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Container and Dockerfile", b: "A container is an application plus its dependencies isolated in one box — an image built from a Dockerfile that runs identically anywhere Docker exists. The Dockerfile is the recipe: a base image, copying the code in, installing the dependencies, and declaring the run command. The distinction to keep straight: the image is the blueprint, the container is the running instance of that blueprint. For this project the containers are the API (with the agent) and the database." },
    { h: "docker-compose: several services, one command", b: "docker-compose defines several services — api and db — in a compose.yaml file and starts them together. The three commands the professor needs: docker compose up -d (start everything in the background), docker compose logs -f (watch the logs live), and docker compose down (stop everything). One compose file, one command, whole project running — that is the 'docker compose up and it works' pitch from the qbank, and it removes every 'works on my machine' excuse from the demo." },
    { h: "Volumes: the classic demo disaster", b: "Volumes are persistent storage outside the container filesystem. Without a volume, your SQLite file and uploaded receipts vanish the moment the container is recreated — the classic student demo disaster: 'it worked yesterday'. The paths that MUST be volumes in your compose file: the database file, the receipts folder, and .env. With volumes, the data survives container recreation, which is exactly what happens when the professor restarts the demo machine." },
    { h: "Best practices and the fallback", b: "Best practices for the demo: pin image versions (no floating tags), keep secrets in .env and gitignore it (AI API key, SECRET_KEY, DB path — never committed), one compose file for the whole stack, and a healthcheck for the database so the API waits for it to be ready. If Docker is impossible on the grading machine, the fallback is a venv plus requirements.txt plus a run script — still one command to start, still no excuses." },
    { h: "Common pitfalls", b: "Pitfall one, the classic: no volume for the SQLite file — the container recreates, the data vanishes, and the demo dies with 'it worked yesterday'. Pitfall two: docker compose down -v, which deletes volumes deliberately; teach the professor the plain down, and never put -v in the demo instructions. Pitfall three: committing .env with the AI API key — the key is a live credential and the repo may be shared. The three volume paths from the sub-questions — DB file, receipts folder, .env — cover the data and the secrets; everything else can rebuild.\n\nPitfall four: unpinned image versions (latest) — a rebuild weeks later pulls a different image and the demo changes behavior for no reason; pin the exact tags. Pitfall five: no healthcheck for the database — the API starts before the DB is ready, and the first request fails in front of the professor. Pitfall six: one container for everything — the point of compose is separate api and db services with volumes between them. Logs are the debugging path: docker compose logs -f shows the API and DB output together, which is where the professor will look first on a failure.\n\nThe recreate test is the demo's insurance: docker compose down, then up -d, then check that the data is still there. Run it before the demo, not during. And write the fallback (venv + requirements.txt + run script) in your answer only if the grading machine truly cannot run Docker — otherwise one compose file is the deliverable. Decide the container names and ports now, and write them in the answer — the demo instructions should never say 'the port in the docs'." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Which paths MUST be volumes in your compose file?", info: "DB file, receipts folder, .env." },
    { q: "Write the three commands the professor needs.", info: "docker compose up -d, docker compose logs -f, docker compose down." },
    { q: "What is in your .env and what is gitignored?", info: "AI API key, SECRET_KEY, DB path — never committed." }
  ],
  links: [
    "Docker docs — https://docs.docker.com/",
    "Docker Compose docs — https://docs.docker.com/compose/",
    "TASKS.md — Step 6 (repo)"
  ]
};
PAGES.q6_10 = {
  intro: "The study list is a real deliverable: TASKS.md Step 6 expects you to understand AI-DLC, OpenCode, Antigravity, and Docker basics in 2-3 days of learning. This page turns that vague goal into specific, answerable questions you can work through when you are back online — each one maps to a tool row in the Step 6 checklist and ticking it later is the proof.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Why the list exists", b: "TASKS.md Step 6 has four tool rows: AI-DLC, OpenCode, Antigravity, and Docker basics — and the trip is the scheduled study time. Vague goals ('learn Docker') fail because they have no done-criterion; specific questions ('how do Docker volumes persist a SQLite file?') produce concrete answers you can write down and verify. The post-trip OpenCode prompt turns your answers into the study checklist, so the questions you park here become the boxes you tick later." },
    { h: "The 'compare X vs Y for Z' format", b: "Comparison questions produce concrete decisions instead of summaries: aiogram 3 vs python-telegram-bot for Telegram Mini Apps; mmdc (mermaid-cli) — how it installs (Node + Chromium) and how it exports diagrams; Docker volumes for SQLite persistence; Antigravity vs OpenCode as the project cockpit; current Whisper vs GPT-4o-transcribe pricing for the audio pipeline. Each question ends with a decision or a number, which is what makes it answerable in one focused reading session." },
    { h: "The candidate questions, with context", b: "Five candidates cover the Step 6 rows and the build pipeline. (1) Which Telegram Python library — aiogram 3 (async) vs python-telegram-bot — for a bot with Mini Apps? (2) How does mermaid-cli install and export? (3) How do Docker volumes persist the SQLite file and receipts? (4) How does Antigravity compare to OpenCode for running the AGENTS.md workflow? (5) What do Whisper and GPT-4o-transcribe cost per minute now? Each maps to a row in TASKS.md Step 6 or to a decision the stack will need." },
    { h: "Feasibility and order", b: "At roughly 30 minutes per question, 5-6 questions is about half a day of focused reading — comfortably inside the 2-3 day budget Step 6 allows. The order matters: learn OpenCode FIRST, because it is the tool that will build the project; Docker second, because the demo depends on it; then Antigravity and the library comparisons. Keep the list inside the trip export so the post-trip run can turn it into the study checklist automatically." },
    { h: "Turning answers into checklist ticks", b: "The research list only pays off if the answers land somewhere the workflow can read. Write each answer into the trip export as you finish it (one line per question, with the source link), and keep the export JSON daily per TRIP-GUIDE.md. The post-trip OpenCode run maps your answers to the Step 6 rows and updates TASKS.md — an answer recorded today is a checkbox closed next week. The daily export is also the backup rule: if the phone dies, the answers from yesterday survive — the same rule as the architecture answers (q5_5).\n\nKeep evidence light but real: the link, one sentence of what you learned, and the decision it produced ('aiogram 3 — async, Mini App support, active maintenance'). Three items per tool is enough; a wall of notes is not read. 'Understood' means you can explain the tool to the professor in two sentences, not that you memorized its docs. The 'compare X vs Y for Z' shape (qbank hint) is what makes each answer concrete — decide, don't summarize.\n\nOrder the list by dependency: OpenCode first (it builds the project), Docker second (the demo depends on it), then the library comparisons and Antigravity. With 30 minutes per question, five or six questions fit in half a day — well inside the 2-3 days TASKS.md allocates. Write the order in your answer so the post-trip run does not have to guess. And keep the questions themselves in the export now, even unanswered — the list is the deliverable, the answers come later. Audio clips count too: a 30-second voice note per question is a fine answer format for the Step 6 notes." },
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Write 3 questions, one per tool, in 'compare/decide' form.", info: "'Decide X vs Y for Z' questions produce concrete answers." },
    { q: "Which tool will you learn FIRST when online?", info: "OpenCode — it is the one building your project." },
    { q: "Estimate 30 min per question — is the list feasible in 2-3 days?", info: "5-6 questions ≈ half a day of focused reading." }
  ],
  links: [
    "TASKS.md — Step 6 (repo)",
    "OpenCode docs — https://opencode.ai/docs",
    "Google Antigravity — https://antigravity.google/"
  ]
};
